title: Baptist Perpetuity
description: Witnesses from outside the Baptists, Catholic, Lutheran, Presbyterian, Methodist, Quaker and Reformed, on Baptist churches existing through the centuries before the Reformation and apart from Rome.
output: lessons/history/baptist-perpetuity.html

# Built from the notes studies/data/notes/baptist-perpetuity-notes.html, in their order; each quotation keeps the source the notes give.

=== Introduction
--- Baptist Perpetuity [title]
overline: Baptist & church history
heading: Baptist Perpetuity
sub: The Baptists are the only known body of Christians that has not symbolized with the Church of Rome. (Sir Isaac Newton)
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any link to open the lesson.

=== Not symbolized with Rome
--- Sir Isaac Newton
quote: The Baptists are the only known body of Christians that has not symbolized with the Church of Rome. | Sir Isaac Newton · The First Church, J. T. Mann

--- The true Church
quote: “They professed to be the true Church, because they followed Christ, and patterned after the Apostles; they sought no secular gain or earthly property, but were the poor in Christ, while the Roman Church made itself rich. They accounted themselves as sheep amongst wolves, fleeing from city to city, enduring persecution with the ancient martyrs, although they were living laborious, holy and self-denying lives.… They rejected the intercession of saints, and they called all observances in the Church which Christ had not established superstitions. They denied the doctrine of purgatorial fire after death, and believed that when men die they go immediately to heaven or to hell.” | Catholic Everine on the Cathari (a Baptist group in the 12th century)

=== Before Luther and Calvin
--- Mosheim (Lutheran)
quote: Before the rise of Luther and Calvin, there lay secreted in almost all the countries of Europe persons who adhered tenaciously to the principles of modern Dutch Baptists. | Mosheim (Lutheran) · The Baptist Spirit, Isaac J. Van Ness, William D. Nowlin

--- Cardinal Hosius (Catholic, 1524)
kicker: President of the Council of Trent
quote: “Were it not that the Baptists have been grievously tormented and cut off with the knife during the past twelve hundred years, they would swarm in greater number than all the Reformers.” | Hosius, Letters, Apud Opera, p. 112, 113.
callout: The “twelve hundred years” were the years preceding the Reformation in which Rome persecuted Baptists with the most cruel persecution thinkable.

--- Edinburgh Cyclopedia (Presbyterian)
quote: “It must have already occurred to our readers that the Baptists are the same sect of Christians that were formerly described as Ana-Baptists. Indeed this seems to have been their leading principle from the time of Tertullian to the present time.” | Edinburgh Cyclopedia (Presbyterian) · The Trail of Blood, J. M. Carroll

=== All Christians were then Baptists
--- John Clark Ridpath (Methodist)
quote: “I should not readily admit that there was a Baptist Church as far back as A. D. 100, though without doubt there were Baptists then, as all Christians were then Baptists.” | John Clark Ridpath (Methodist) · Cole C.D. Definitions of Doctrine, Volume III, The New Testament Church

--- Professor Wm. C. Duncan
kicker: Department of Greek and Latin, University of Louisiana
quote: “Baptists do not, as most Protestant denominations, date their origin from the Reformation of 1520. By means of that great movement they were brought out of comparative obscurity into prominent notice. They did not, however, originate with the Reformation; for long before Luther lived; yea, long before the Catholic Church itself was known, Baptists and Baptist churches flourished in Europe, Asia and Africa.” | Professor Wm. C. Duncan · Moser, M. L. Jr. Baptist Handbook for Church Members (Revised), Little Rock, Arkansas: Challenge Press, 1983, p. 46.

=== From the times of the apostles
--- Zwingli (1525)
kicker: The Swiss reformer, contemporary with Luther and Calvin
quote: “The institution of Anabaptism is no novelty but for thirteen hundred years has caused great disturbances in the church and has acquired such a strength that the attempt in this age to contend with it appeared futile for a while.” | Zwingli · Dayton, Amos Cooper. Pedobaptist and Campbellite Immersions. Southwestern Publishing House, 1858, p. 150.
note: (By the “church” Zwingli means the Roman Catholic church.)

--- Robert Barclay (Quaker)
quote: “We shall afterwards show the rise of the Anabaptists took place prior to the Reformation of the Church of England, and there are also reasons for believing that on the Continent of Europe small hidden Christian societies, who have held many of the opinions of the Anabaptists, have existed from the times of the apostles.” | Robert Barclay (Quaker) · Christian, John T. History of the Baptists together with Some Account of Their Principles and Practices. Nashville, TN: Baptist Sunday School Committee of the American Baptist Association, 1922

--- William C. King
quote: “Of the Baptists it may be said that they are not Reformers. These people, comprising bodies of Christian believers, known under various names in different countries, are entirely independent of and distinct from the Greek and Roman churches, and have an unbroken continuity from apostolic days down through the centuries. Throughout this long period they were bitterly persecuted for heresy, driven from country to country, disfranchised, deprived of their property, imprisoned, tortured and slain by the thousands; yet they swerved not from the New Testament faith, doctrine and practice.” | King, William C. Crossing the Centuries. Springfield, MA: Editorial Associates, 1912

